comment_grading:
Not yet graded. The teacher says absolute grading is used this term, 90-100: A; 80-90: B; 70-80: C.
Grading scheme varies from time to time, as there were no such scheme. But Approximately:
Attendance & Participation 25%;
Case Study 30% includes:
   It's checkpoint 1%,
   It's discussion 5% and
   Presentation in video 24%;
In Class Quizzes (~8) 15%;
Midterm 20%;
Final 25%.
Based on the quizzes, the terms to remember rather to understand made the quizzes hard. Teachers won't release the exact the schedule, you have only around 1 day to prepare.
Teachers releases the grades very passively. You won't be able to see your grades for a long while.

comment_teaching:
three hour lecture is so unbearable so can skip all;
better rely on the 50min tutorial which doesn't happen every week and one TA gives really good practice problems while the other just talks quietly;
and mostly rely on AI and making really good cheat sheet for ur midterm and final;

comment_grading:
Unclear grading criteria for group projects, the exam of reciting, and bad overall grading as SSC-CCC.
a) a mid-term exam (covering the first four lectures) (30%); 
b) a project presentation (20%); 
c) a project essay (20%); 
d) a final exam (covering the last four lectures) (30%). 
I got 86 out of 100 in midterm (more than 1 SD above mean), 74 out of 100 in final (mean: 70.65),  93 out of 100 in presentation (upper: 91, high: 95), unknown grading for essay and end up with B+.
The most disgusting thing is that they open for paper checking but the only explanation is this is the objective answer, you need to accept. But actually, some of the answers are contradicted  from what Prof. has said in the course and TA will say he can't remember. Other answers like fill in the blanks, if you use similar wording but not the exact word on the PPT, TA will say he remembers the exact word Prof. used and you are wrong.
For group projects, there are no clear grading criteria.

